    "updated_at": "2022-07-30T02:29:46Z",
    "body": "MDN reference stating the send method enqueues data: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebSocket/send\r\n\r\nSome scenarios:\r\n\r\nIf a send method is executed on a web socket and then another send method is executed on that socket a few lines down within the same function there will be a collision.  The JavaScript language will execute the instructions faster than network hardware can process these instructions into messages over the network.  When this collision occurs the first payload will be abandoned and only the second payload will be sent.  I have verified this on both Firefox and Chrome.\r\n\r\nIf a web socket is sending media and while that media is sending any use of a send method upon that socket will halt the media transfer by imposing a collision.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nI have known for a while the data is not queued in Firefox and two days ago I discovered the data is also never queued in Chrome even if the payloads are tiny and even if they are delayed from each other by a few milliseconds.\r\n\r\nThese collisions, or even the potential thereof, is the biggest limitation of web sockets.  It is a great loss of network fidelity that is easily reproducible.  The point of this issue is that with the proper tools available this problem can be easily managed and mitigated.  I have already solved this issue in a web socket implementation I wrote for Node.js.  It would benefit everybody if the proper signaling were equally available in the browser.",
